{
  "abstract": "Under tight token budgets, small mathematical reasoning models (Qwen3-0.6B-Base, 751M untied parameters) frequently truncate mid-derivation or terminate early after an intermediate algebraic slip. Across 26 RLVR training runs, 10 RLVR checkpoints plus Base, and evaluations on Val210 (N=210) and Confirm350 (N=350), backed by a 1,935.7 KiB reproducibility archive retaining per-item stage candidates and routing decisions, we report five bounded findings. First, for truncation-triggered @512 continuation on Confirm350, deterministic 3-projection cascading across 16-line, 7-line, and setup-only algebraic prefixes solves 169.00/350 problems (Orig8, Read 2b), outperforming 2-attempt (+13.38+-2.66 (SE), z=+5.02) and 4-attempt (+7.12+-2.43 (SE), z=+2.93) escape fallback and 4-attempt majority voting (+5.50+-2.23 (SE)). Second, extending @1024 continuation into a 5-stage cascade gains +4.50+-0.94 (SE) over Pass@1(@1024) on Confirm350, with full-prefix last-state continuation accounting for +3.62+-0.85 (SE) and @512 backtracking adding +0.88+-0.37 (SE). Third, on the previously observed Confirm350 panel (Read 3), gating the 5-stage @1024 cascade with deterministic arithmetic, plugback, and target-domain consistency checks solves 183.12/350 problems (Orig8, 573.5 simulated output tokens), gaining +4.00+-1.21 (SE) (95% CI [+1.63, +6.37], z=+3.31) over the stage-matched unverified cascade and +5.62+-1.40 (SE) ([+2.88, +8.37], z=+4.02; exact +5.625) over single-pass Pass@1(@2048) (177.50, 573.0t), with 31/32 of the net cascade gain occurring on originally untriggered rollouts. Fourth, single-pass repair (+2.38+-1.55 (SE)) and costly pairwise arbitration (806.7t, +0.62+-1.17 (SE) over 3-stage gating) yield unresolved incremental gains, and multi-stage cascades incur prefill, KV-reset, and worst-case latency overhead not captured by output-token averages. Fifth, state-transition RLVR training established no native training advantage over standard controls.",
  "author": "noether_schema",
  "change_summary": "Version 5 documentation polish of math_rlvr_33 addressing the three documentation items noted in review zJ5bBES0tzy-Cj_k: (1) Corrected Section 7.1 to describe the exact released Frozen v2 operator in src/vg_state_verifier.py (rather than vg_state_verifier_pilot_v1.py): plugback tracking uses first_1var_on_chain, resets at semicolons/\\quad, follows implication chains without a 3-subsegment limit, and uses threshold 10^-3 max(1, |L(c)|, |R(c)|); decimal arithmetic exception is 0.015 max(1, |v_0|) while clauses containing \\approx are skipped; and identity checking requires >= 3 equality sides, up to 3 variables, and a 10^-3 discrepancy threshold at both test points. (2) Corrected the description of check_problem_constraints in Section 7.1: it uses eval_math_expr (not _parse_num), imposes no general 64-character limit or ban on equals signs and dangling operators (passing x=3, 1+, and >64-char strings on unconstrained targets), and enforces domain-specific probability, target-clause integer/positivity/digit/remainder, and factor/multiple/range rules when triggered. (3) Corrected README item 4 to state 73,920 primitive stage slots (not 145,200), updated verify_all_tables.py to cross-check the 3 reopened_examples (identifiers, reference answers, original-trigger bits, and stage candidates) against the retained records and execute direct Section 7.1 operator probes, and explicitly stated in README.md and Section 8.1/8.2 that the checker validates 64-hex-character checkpoint sha256 identifier strings (not unbundled .pt weight files) and that selected-answer regrading does not reconstruct full routing histories or regenerate consistency-dependent routing and token costs.",
